  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24–48h and May 20–27 gap)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>
        On May 23–24, a burst of US-side diplomatic statements materially advanced the Iran-US negotiating picture. Trump told reporters the deal was "largely negotiated" (NPR, May 23). Secretary of State Rubio confirmed on May 24 there was "a pretty solid thing on the table" covering Hormuz opening and a "real significant time-limited negotiation on nuclear matters" (Al Jazeera, CNN). Axios published the proposed MOU's core terms May 24: Hormuz fully reopens during a 60-day window; Iran commits to no-nuclear-weapons; the US commits to negotiate sanctions relief. Neither a signing nor a co-confirmation by Iranian principals had occurred as of May 27 reporting. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span>, <span class="cite">N2</span>
      </p>
      <p>
        On May 25, Iranian FM Araghchi and Parliament Speaker Ghalibaf arrived in Doha for talks described as covering "issues relating to the Strait (of Hormuz) and highly enriched uranium," with central bank chief Hemmati examining the possible release of Iranian assets frozen in Qatar (Al Arabiya, The National, May 25). The simultaneous presence of the central bank chief alongside the FM and Speaker is a concrete institutional-preparation step. However, Araghchi stated May 27 he was unsure whether a deal was imminent, and Khamenei's advisor Shamkhani dismissed US nuclear-control terms as a "fantasy" (Al Jazeera, CBS). The two Iranian positions — active preparatory logistics alongside public hedging on core terms — are not contradictory; they are a familiar negotiating posture from prior rounds. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move
      </p>
      <p>
        The Knesset dissolution bill passed a preliminary reading of 110-0 on May 20, with no specific election date specified (Haaretz, Al Jazeera). Both coalition and opposition submitted separate dissolution bills; analysts described this as Netanyahu and the Haredim "jostling for leverage, not a ballot date" (Times of Israel). The bill requires committee deliberation and two further plenary readings before becoming law, and Netanyahu has stated a preference for an October date — within the existing constitutional window. No formal election date was announced by Netanyahu as of May 27. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span>
      </p>
      <p>
        The Lebanon ceasefire reached its first formal test — the F4 horizon of May 22 — without a qualifying collapse event. IDF strikes in southern Lebanon continued through May 22 (Soufan Center, May 22 brief), including the killing of six paramedics and civilians in Deir Qanoun an-Naher, Nabatieh and Kfar Sir on May 19. Hezbollah fired rockets in retaliation. Israeli National Security Minister Ben-Gvir on May 25 called for a return to "large-scale combat." The Washington Post reported on May 26 the ceasefire was "faltering." Despite all this, neither the US State Department nor the Lebanese government issued a formal withdrawal-of-sponsorship statement. The US-sponsored security track remains scheduled for the Pentagon on May 29 and political talks June 2–3 (CNBC, PBS, GlobalSecurity). → moved <span class="cite">F4</span>, supports <span class="cite">N3</span> without move
      </p>
      <p>
        Commercial shipping through the Strait of Hormuz remained effectively closed as of the most recent IMF PortWatch data (via MacroMicro, May 17), showing transits at approximately 2% of pre-crisis baseline — around 2 vessels against a pre-war figure of ~95 per day. Maersk, MSC, CMA CGM, and Hapag-Lloyd remain suspended. US Navy Operation Project Freedom was paused May 6 after escorting only two US-flagged vessels. Lloyd's war-risk premium remained at multi-percent of hull value, a level the Lloyd's Market Association described as commercially prohibitive — with the LMA noting safety, not insurance availability, is the binding constraint. The 50/day threshold required for F1 resolution is a ~25x increase from current levels. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

