  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        Iranian state media ran two parallel framings this week that did not contradict each other. IRNA and Press TV covered Araghchi's and Ghalibaf's Doha trip (May 25) in factual, non-triumphalist terms — noting the visit covered "Strait and highly enriched uranium issues" without previewing an agreement. IRIB's domestic broadcasts gave more space to the Shamkhani formulation ("fantasy") on US nuclear demands, which appeared intended for a domestic audience skeptical of any deal that constrains the nuclear program. Fars News Agency gave the largest prominence to the framing that the US had "agreed in principle" to Iranian asset releases and sanctions talks — an element of the MOU that is favorable in Iranian domestic political terms. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move
      </p>
      <p>
        Tasnim and Kayhan ran extended commentary on what they framed as US "violations" of the existing ceasefire — citing overnight US strikes on May 25-26 reported by CBS as a "grave violation" — and called for Araghchi to condition any deal signing on cessation of strikes. This framing establishes a domestic political cover for Khamenei's office to accept or reject the MOU on procedural grounds rather than on the substance of nuclear terms. Press TV also ran a segment on the Hezbollah-Lebanon track, describing the ceasefire as a "US project to protect Israeli occupation" — consistent with the Resistance Axis framing that has been standard since April 16. → <span class="context-only">context only</span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

